# Colour gradients: scale_gradient

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`scale_colour_gradient()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/scale_gradient.html), which
covers `scale_fill_gradient()`, `scale_colour_gradient2()`, `scale_fill_gradient2()`,
`scale_colour_gradientn()` and `scale_fill_gradientn()` too, in the order the page gives them.
Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps
its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

A gradient scale maps a continuous column to colour, or to fill, by blending between colours.
`scale_colour_gradient()` blends from a low colour to a high one, `scale_colour_gradient2()`
diverges from a low colour through a middle one to a high one, around a midpoint, and
`scale_colour_gradientn()` blends through as many colours as it is given; the `fill` versions do the
same for fill. A missing value is drawn in grey, or in the colour `na.value` gives. The legend that
reads a gradient back is a colour bar, `guide_colourbar()`. Giggly colours a continuous column with
ggplot2's default gradient, `scale_colour_gradient()`'s, from `#132B43` to `#56B1F7`, a missing
value in grey, and reads it back with a colour bar, but it has no scale functions yet, so none of
their options.

## The default gradient

The examples draw from two tables of random numbers R makes after `set.seed(1)`: `df`, and
`df_na`, whose `z1` is missing in its first ten rows.

```r
set.seed(1)
df <- data.frame(
  x = runif(100),
  y = runif(100),
  z1 = rnorm(100),
  z2 = abs(rnorm(100))
)

df_na <- data.frame(
  value = seq(1, 20),
  x = runif(20),
  y = runif(20),
  z1 = c(rep(NA, 10), rnorm(10))
)

# Default colour scale colours from light blue to dark blue
ggplot(df, aes(x, y)) +
  geom_point(aes(colour = z2))
```

The cell writes out `df`'s `x`, `y` and `z2` as R made them, each number to the last digit R
holds. A column of numbers mapped to `colour` takes ggplot2's default gradient, from `#132B43` at
the smallest `z2` to `#56B1F7` at the largest, blended in L\*a\*b\*, and a colour bar reads it
back.

